= Acknowledgements <acknowledgements>

I would like to thank my supervisors, Alexandra Graß and Helmut Seidl, for
their guidance and support throughout this thesis. I am especially grateful to
Alexandra for the many discussions, detailed feedback, and ideas that shaped
the project, and I greatly appreciated the freedom to explore the topic in
depth and to follow directions that emerged along the way. Javier Esparza kindly
agreed to examine this thesis.

I am also grateful to Michael Petter for helping to scope the project and shape
its initial direction, and to Julian Erhard for ideas and feedback that brought
the formalization closer to Goblint. I also thank Sarah Tilscher for developing
and maintaining the verified TD solver formalization on which this work builds.

I also acknowledge the many contributors to Goblint, whose work on the analyzer
provided both a practical reference point and much of the inspiration for this
thesis, as well as the developers of Isabelle and the open-source tooling
around it. In particular, the Isabelle interfaces offered by AutoCorrode and
Kevin Kappelmann's MCP server made it practical to integrate AI-assisted
development into a formalization of this scale.

I am also grateful to the members of the Chair for Programming Languages,
Compiler Construction and Specification Formalisms. I first came to know the
chair through the course Functional Programming and Verification, and it became
an important part of my time at TUM. Working there twice as a teaching
assistant and once as a tutor gave me insight into both teaching and research,
and showed me how much I enjoy teaching formal methods.

Finally, I would like to thank my family and friends. My family gave me the
freedom to focus fully on my work and encouraged me through every stage of my
studies. My friends provided encouragement, welcome distractions, and regular
reminders that there is a world outside Isabelle, and their company made the
more intense periods of work much easier. Their patience, friendship, and
support have meant a great deal to me throughout my time at TUM.
